# eu-u9xj.6 (PP) fixture for test_pp_gc_collects_during_par_map in
# harness_test.rs — the GC-safety gate for the parallel driver.
#
# The driver has to hold heap handles across `force()` calls: mapping N
# elements means accumulating N results while forcing each one. A handle on
# the Rust stack is invisible to the collector, so if the driver kept them
# there, a collection during the map would move the objects out from under it
# and leave it dereferencing freed or relocated memory. They live in the
# machine's root set instead (`IntrinsicMachine::gc_root_push`).
#
# `EU_GC_VERIFY=2` cannot see that class of bug — it verifies reachability
# *from roots*, and the whole point is that these handles were not roots. What
# catches it is running the map with collection actually happening during it,
# under `EU_GC_STRESS=1` so every collection evacuates rather than merely
# marking, and comparing the answer with the `map` oracle beside this file.
#
# The shape is tuned so a collection lands mid-map: under the small heap
# limit the test passes, the collection trigger (allocation volume since the
# last collection, capped at the headroom below the limit — eu-oaz72.4) is
# reached partway through, and each element allocates a 60-element
# intermediate list. Raise the limit enough and the run finishes without
# collecting at all and the gate stops gating. Re-verified after eu-oaz72.4
# changed when collections happen: holding the results on the Rust stack
# still fails the test. If this fixture is ever retuned, re-verify by fault
# injection that it still fails when the root set is removed.

f(x): range(0, 60) map((_0 + x)) sum
xs: range(1, 30)
r: xs par-map(f)
n: r sum
